<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Trinovantes</h1><p class="st-meta">54 BCE – 43 CE (approx.) · capital Camulodunum (Colchester)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Essex people whose alliance with Julius Caesar made them Rome's first British treaty partners, and who later rose with Boudica against Camulodunum.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Trinovantes first appear in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s account of 54 BCE, when their prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandubracius">Mandubracius</a>, whose father had been killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassivellaunus">Cassivellaunus</a>, fled to Caesar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Caesar restored him and forbade Cassivellaunus to harm them, making the Trinovantes the earliest British people bound to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by agreement.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their land in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-essex">Essex</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a> was rich farmland with close links to the Continent. Rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addedomarus">Addedomarus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubnovellaunus">Dubnovellaunus</a> issued gold coins, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a> grew into a vast oppidum defended by long dykes, with burials like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lexden-tumulus">Lexden tumulus</a> holding Roman luxury goods. Around 10 CE the Catuvellaunian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunobelinus">Cunobelinus</a> took Camulodunum and made it his capital.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Claudian invasion of 43, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a> became the first capital of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a> and from 49 a colony of veterans who seized Trinovantian land. Resentment led the Trinovantes to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/iceni">Iceni</a> in 60 or 61, when Camulodunum was burned. Afterwards they formed a civitas in the Roman province.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/trinovantes.jpg" alt="Trinovantes" width="320" height="396"><figcaption>Image: Jpb1301 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Trinovantes.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>54 BCE – 43 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Camulodunum (Colchester)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mandubracius, Addedomarus, Dubnovellaunus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Trinobantes</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-6&amp;focus=trinovantes" aria-label="Trinovantes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4700 384 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Trinovantes lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5020 640L5001 643M5020 640L5304 942M5020 640L5020 640"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5001 643h0M5304 942h0M5020 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5020 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5020 640h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Camulodunum. <a href="/atlas?year=-6&amp;focus=trinovantes">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
